(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-5-blocks-ventricular-arrhythmias-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Arrhythmias II: Blocks and Ventriculars Quiz 2 — CMS I Exam 5","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 5","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"For which patients with first-degree atrioventricular block should a permanent pacemaker be considered?","choices":["Anyone taking digoxin","Patients with Lyme disease","Symptomatic patients","Everyone over age 65"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A permanent pacemaker is considered for patients whose first-degree block causes symptoms, typically once the PR interval exceeds 0.3 seconds; otherwise no treatment is needed. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Which finding defines third-degree (complete) atrioventricular block?","choices":["Prolonged but constant PR interval","P waves and QRS complexes unrelated","PR lengthening until a QRS drops","Constant PR with random dropped QRS"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In complete block no P waves are conducted, so the atria and ventricles are completely dissociated and beat at two different regular rates. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Why is Mobitz type II block treated as dangerous even without symptoms?","choices":["Leads to atrial fibrillation","Signals digoxin toxicity","Unpredictable progression","Frequent heart failure"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Guidelines treat Mobitz type II as dangerous even without symptoms because of its unpredictable progression to complete heart block and its poor prognosis. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"When right bundle branch block causes symptoms, what do they usually point to?","choices":["Severe underlying heart disease","A harmless normal variant","An accessory pathway","Isolated electrolyte loss"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Right bundle branch block is usually asymptomatic; patients who develop symptoms usually have severe underlying cardiac disease, with heart failure-like symptoms. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"Which feature separates Mobitz type I from Mobitz type II block?","choices":["Whether the QRS complexes are narrow","Whether the atrial rate is regular","Whether the PR lengthens before a drop","Whether any P waves fail to conduct"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Mobitz type I shows a progressively lengthening PR interval before the dropped QRS; Mobitz type II keeps a constant PR and drops QRS complexes at random. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Which electrocardiogram findings define asystole?","choices":["Wide beats at 30 a minute","No rate, rhythm, P or QRS","Chaotic, uncoordinated waves","Organized beats, no pulse"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Asystole (ventricular standstill) has a rate of zero, no rhythm, no P waves, no PR interval and no QRS complexes. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"Besides old age, which chronic conditions drive the degenerative His-Purkinje disease behind Mobitz type II block?","choices":["Asthma and eczema","Gout and osteoarthritis","Migraine and anxiety","Hypertension and diabetes"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Degenerative His-Purkinje disease, the most common cause of Mobitz type II, comes especially from old age, chronic hypertension and diabetes. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which infiltrative diseases are named causes of third-degree (complete) atrioventricular block?","choices":["Asthma and emphysema","Amyloidosis and sarcoidosis","Cirrhosis and pancreatitis","Osteoporosis and gout"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Inflammatory and infiltrative disease, namely myocarditis, amyloidosis and sarcoidosis, is listed among the causes of complete heart block. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Which kind of shock is delivered for polymorphic ventricular tachycardia?","choices":["Synchronized cardioversion","A low-energy timed shock","No shock, only drugs","Unsynchronized defibrillation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Polymorphic ventricular tachycardia is never truly stable and its QRS shape keeps changing, so it is defibrillated (unsynchronized) even when a pulse is present. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"Which inflammatory conditions are named causes of Mobitz type II block?","choices":["Gastritis and esophagitis","Sinusitis and bronchitis","Myocarditis, reactive arthritis","Pericarditis and pleurisy"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Inflammatory disease such as myocarditis and reactive arthritis is listed among the causes of Mobitz type II, with His-Purkinje degeneration, Lyme carditis, infarction and ablation. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which is the most common predisposing condition for sudden cardiac death?","choices":["Coronary artery disease","Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy","Long QT syndrome","An accessory pathway"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Coronary artery disease is the most common predisposing condition for sudden cardiac death, even in patients without a history of myocardial infarction. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"Which external device detects and shocks fatal ventricular arrhythmias while worn on the body?","choices":["Wearable defibrillator vest","Implantable loop recorder","Ambulatory Holter monitor","Transcutaneous pacing pads"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. There is a wearable (external) cardioverter defibrillator, worn as a vest, that detects fatal ventricular arrhythmias and delivers a shock. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"Procainamide should be avoided in stable wide QRS tachycardia when which is present?","choices":["Prolonged QT or heart failure","Asthma or chronic bronchitis","Hypothyroidism or anemia","Diabetes or obesity"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Procainamide is avoided when the QT is prolonged or the patient has congestive heart failure, because it lengthens the QT and depresses the heart. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"Which is the most common cause of first-degree atrioventricular block?","choices":["Atrioventricular node ischemia","Overactive vagal tone","Age-related conduction degeneration","Lyme disease of the heart"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Age-related degenerative change of the conduction system is the most common cause: the pathway wears out, so the impulse takes longer to cross the atrioventricular node. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"In third-degree (complete) atrioventricular block, where can the non-conducted P waves be found?","choices":["Nowhere: atria are silent","Inverted after each QRS","Buried in QRS, ST segment or T wave","Only before each QRS complex"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The P waves keep their uniform shape and regular rate, with no association to the QRS complexes, so they may be buried in the QRS complex, ST segment or T wave. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Why are beta blockers avoided in drug-induced torsades de pointes?","choices":["They interact with magnesium","Bradycardia lengthens the QT","They cause hypokalemia","They shorten the QT too much"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Beta blockers cause bradycardia and lengthen the R to R interval, which indirectly prolongs the QT and worsens acquired torsades. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"What are the mainstays of treatment for asystole and pulseless electrical activity?","choices":["Defibrillation and amiodarone","Synchronized cardioversion","Magnesium and lidocaine","Compressions and epinephrine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. High-quality chest compressions and epinephrine by intravenous or intraosseous push at intervals are the mainstays, with early intubation considered. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"When left bundle branch block does cause symptoms, what do they resemble?","choices":["Panic attack","Hypoglycemia","Heart failure","Asthma attack"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Symptomatic left bundle branch block resembles a congestive heart failure exacerbation, with dizziness, shortness of breath and dyspnea on exertion. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"Which electrolyte is given as an intravenous push and then as an infusion to break torsades de pointes?","choices":["Magnesium","Potassium","Calcium","Phosphate"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In a conscious patient, intravenous magnesium breaks torsades de pointes, and more magnesium is then given as an infusion; it is the main treatment. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"Which complication of bacterial endocarditis is a named cause of third-degree (complete) atrioventricular block?","choices":["Glomerulonephritis","An associated abscess","Splinter hemorrhages","Septic lung emboli"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Bacterial endocarditis with an associated abscess is listed among the causes of complete block, because the infection can extend into the conduction system. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Which premature ventricular contraction burden puts a patient in the high-risk group?","choices":["Over 1% of beats","Over 0.5% of beats","Any single ectopic","Over 15% of beats"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A high burden, over 15% of beats or 15,000 a day, is one of the high-risk features, with premature ventricular contraction-induced cardiomyopathy, structural or conduction disease and syncope. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Which electrocardiogram finding defines first-degree atrioventricular block?","choices":["PR lengthening until a QRS drops","Constant PR with random dropped QRS","P waves unrelated to the QRS","Constant PR interval over 0.20 seconds"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. First-degree atrioventricular block is a prolonged PR interval, greater than 0.20 seconds and constant, with one P wave for every narrow QRS complex. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"Which valve disease is singled out as a cause of left bundle branch block?","choices":["Aortic stenosis","Mitral stenosis","Pulmonic stenosis","Tricuspid regurgitation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Valvular heart disease, especially aortic stenosis, is a named cause of left bundle branch block, along with chronic hypertension, ischemic heart disease and dilated cardiomyopathy. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"Because Mobitz type II block usually originates at the bundle of His, what is true of its escape rhythm?","choices":["Fast and narrow","Sinus driven","Always over 60","Weak and unreliable"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Because the block sits low, at the bundle of His, the escape rhythm is weak and unreliable, which adds to the risk of sudden death. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which electrolyte disturbances are named triggers of premature ventricular contractions?","choices":["High phosphate, low chloride","Low potassium, low magnesium","High potassium, low sodium","Low calcium, high sodium"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Hypokalemia and hypomagnesemia, along with hypercalcemia, are the electrolyte imbalances named as causes of premature ventricular contractions. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Most torsades de pointes is due to which cause?","choices":["Congenital long QT syndrome","Acute myocardial ischemia","Accessory pathway","Acquired long QT syndrome"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Most torsades de pointes is due to acquired long QT syndrome, from drugs or from hypokalemia and hypocalcemia, rather than the congenital syndrome. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"Which drugs are named for pharmacologic cardioversion of stable monomorphic ventricular tachycardia?","choices":["Metoprolol, verapamil, digoxin","Lidocaine, amiodarone, procainamide","Adenosine, diltiazem, digoxin","Magnesium, calcium, potassium"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. If hemodynamically stable and monomorphic, sustained ventricular tachycardia is cardioverted pharmacologically with intravenous lidocaine, amiodarone or procainamide. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"What is the first treatment for ventricular fibrillation?","choices":["Transcutaneous pacing","Immediate defibrillation","Synchronized cardioversion","Intravenous amiodarone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Ventricular fibrillation is treated as cardiac arrest: start cardiopulmonary resuscitation, give oxygen, attach the monitor and defibrillate (unsynchronized) as soon as possible. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"Which mechanism makes atrioventricular block more common in elderly patients?","choices":["Degenerative conduction disease","Rising vagal tone with age","Age-related Lyme exposure","Shrinking atrial size"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Age-related degenerative change of the conduction system is the most common cause of first-degree block and drives the His-Purkinje disease behind Mobitz type II and complete block. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Which is the definitive treatment for Mobitz type II block?","choices":["Permanent pacemaker","Transcutaneous pacing","Catheter ablation","Implantable defibrillator"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The definitive treatment for Mobitz type II block is a permanent pacemaker, because the block can progress unpredictably to complete heart block and sudden death. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 14"}]};
